# Code owners for the organization profile repository.
#
# This repository is ORGANIZATION INFRASTRUCTURE, not the ninth MCL component.
# It is deliberately absent from the v1.0.0 manifest, the synchronized release
# tags and the canonical GitHub Release: its lifecycle is configuration, not
# protocol release.
#
# It still warrants review, for a reason the eight repositories do not share.
# What is in here is the public front door, and GitHub serves the files below
# as the DEFAULT SECURITY POLICY AND CONTRIBUTING GUIDE for every MCL
# repository that does not define its own. Seven of the eight do not. A silent
# change here therefore changes the published security policy of seven
# repositories at once, without a commit appearing in any of them.
#
# Every line is a REVIEW requirement, not a claim of authorship. It takes
# effect only once the branch ruleset requires code-owner review, which is
# applied after this repository becomes public -- GitHub Free does not offer
# protection on a private repository.
#
# The last matching pattern wins, so the specific paths below override the
# catch-all.

#
# WHY THESE PATHS NAME TWO ACCOUNTS AND NOT ONE.
#
# They named only @0j0bit until 2026-09-12. That deadlocks: GitHub does not let
# anyone approve their own pull request, and the branch ruleset grants no
# bypass to administrators, so a pull request touching one of these paths and
# authored by its sole code owner could never be merged by anyone.
#
# @0j0bit and @sed-boi are two GitHub accounts controlled by the SAME project
# owner. Either account may satisfy the code-owner review requirement for a
# change it did not author -- in practice, changes proposed by the
# mcl-release-governor App and reviewed as @0j0bit. This is account redundancy,
# NOT independent two-person review, and nothing here claims separation of
# duties.
#
# Both accounts have write access, which a code owner must have to count. The
# path lines below name the same accounts as the catch-all, so their present
# effect is documentary: they record which paths are authorities. If the
# catch-all ever widens, they narrow again on their own.

* @0j0bit @sed-boi

# The public front door, and the two files that stand in for seven
# repositories' own.
